{# How to search the user's documents: documents before the web, one call for every form of a
   name, and the query syntax the model gets wrong most often.

   The whole block renders only when `search_collections` is bound. The sentence on
   `read_more` renders only when that tool is bound too. The sentence on the web renders
   only when `web_enabled` is true. `+%}` keeps the newline after a tag that ends a line.
#}
{% if has('search_collections') %}
Searching the user's documents

1. Search the documents first.{% if web_enabled %} Use the web only after the documents, and only for context. Say which facts came from the web.{% endif +%}
2. Search every collection in one call. Leave out collectionname.
3. Put every form of a name or an address into one {{ tool('search_collections') }} call, as its queries list. Put names and phrases in double quotes.
4. OR, AND and NOT are ordinary words in a query. Use | for either and -word to exclude a word.
5. Copy file_hash and collectionname from a result row. Never write a hash yourself.{% if has('read_more') %} If a row has no file_hash, call {{ tool('read_more') }}.{% endif +%}
6. Set a date or size filter only when the user asks for one.
7. Before you say that the documents hold nothing, list each search you ran, its collections and its count.

Example. The user asks for all data on JoeBWilkinson@cs.com. Make one call to {{ tool('search_collections') }} with queries ["JoeBWilkinson@cs.com", "\"Joe Wilkinson\"", "\"Wilkinson, Joe\"", "JoeBWilkinson"]. Then read the documents that the rows name.
{% endif %}
